<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chennai</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Madras</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chennai, formerly Madras, is the capital of the Indian state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coromandel-coast">Coromandel Coast</a>. It grew around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-st-george">Fort St George</a>, founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">English East India Company</a> in 1639–1640, and became the seat of the Madras Presidency, one of the three great presidencies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>. Its army and fleet took part in British campaigns across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> and beyond. The city was officially renamed Chennai in 1996.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chennai.jpg" alt="Chennai" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: jamal haider from india · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chennai_Central.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>English-language education, newspapers and universities founded in 1857 at Calcutta, Bombay and <b>Madras</b> created a new professional middle class.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>According to the Khalimpur plate, the kings of the Bhojas, Matsyas, <b>Madras</b>, Kurus, Yadus, Yavanas, Avanti, Gandhara and Kira attended the ceremony and approved it, a claim that at least shows the scale of Dharmapala’s ambitions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pala-empire">Pala Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…January 1750 Marthanda Varma dedicated the state to Padmanabha, and the kings ruled as his servants; they were conditionally promoted to Kshatriya status through sixteen mahādānams, until in 1848 Lord Dalhousie warned Uttram Tirunal that <b>Madras</b> would take over the administration if the costly practice did not end.</blockquote><a href="/empire/travancore">Travancore · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The site of <b>Madras</b>, where the English built Fort St George in 1639–40, was granted by a local nayaka who served the Aravidu kings of Vijayanagara.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Scholars have read tamiḻ as “our own speech” or “self-speak”, and the Tamil Lexicon of the University of <b>Madras</b> defines it as “sweetness”.</blockquote><a href="/language/tamil">Tamil · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1842 the <b>Madras</b> Board of Revenue began conservation under the botanist Alexander Gibson, the first state management of forests in the world, and in 1855 Lord Dalhousie’s government introduced the first permanent, large-scale forest conservation programme.</blockquote><a href="/movement/environmental-movement">Environmental movement · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
